**Read:** [Design and Manufacturing II — MIT 2.008, 2025](../sources/Design-and-Manufacturing-II-MIT-2025.lingua#r_2FHEWYSEAHBY7X6030TW00F32E), lectures 11–12 and their notes; reuse the statistical and metrology foundations for sampling and uncertainty.

Specification limits describe acceptable output; control limits describe a model of process behavior. A stable process can produce unacceptable parts, and an apparently capable sample can hide a changing process. Keep time order and meaningful subgrouping when searching for causes.

Under a stable normal model with standard deviation σ, C_p=(USL−LSL)/(6σ), while C_pk also includes centering. For limits 9.9–10.1 mm, mean 10.04 mm and σ=0.02 mm, C_p≈1.667 and C_pk=1.0. These are model summaries, not proof of a future rejection rate under arbitrary drift or dependence.

**Work:** Analyze a chronological dimension series with measurement uncertainty and subgroup rationale. Compare common variation with a changed tool or thermal condition. Test the distributional assumptions before converting a capability index to predicted yield. Evaluate a corrective action on later data, retaining failures and production interruptions rather than selecting only convenient samples.

